31	%% ABSTRACT
32	%% ============================================================
33	\begin{abstract}
34	Benchmark scores for large language models are increasingly unreliable due to training data contamination, yet existing detection methods flag contamination without producing corrected scores.
35	We introduce \contcal{}, a two-stage framework that (1)~maps per-item contamination exposure via Reciprocal Rank Fusion of four detection signals, and (2)~feeds these exposure scores into a contamination-augmented four-parameter logistic (4PL) IRT model with per-benchmark contamination susceptibility parameters~$\gamma_k$.
36	On controlled contamination injection experiments across Qwen2.5 (0.5B--7B) and Llama-3.2-1B, \contcal{} recovers uncontaminated benchmark performance with 2.1\pp{} mean absolute error and 92\% credible interval coverage, outperforming all seven baselines including DCR (4.6\pp{}) and linear regression (4.3\pp{}).
37	We provide the first systematic signal$\times$scale$\times$dosage characterization, revealing that Min-K++ and Self-Critique signals are ineffective below ${\sim}1.2$B parameters (AUC$\approx$0.47) yet recover effectiveness at ${\geq}1.5$B (AUC$\geq$0.70).
38	Critically, Min-K++ exhibits \emph{directional reversal} at high contamination (Cohen's $d=-2.67$ at 3B), actively misleading rather than merely failing.
39	Per-benchmark $\gamma_k$ heterogeneity (CV$=$0.52, cross-family $r=0.91$) reveals that contamination susceptibility is a benchmark property, not a model property.
40	Fisher Information analysis establishes structural (AUC$\geq$0.55) and practical (AUC$\geq$0.70) identifiability thresholds, validated by 144-condition simulation and cross-family experiments.
41	\end{abstract}
42	
43	%% ============================================================
44	%% 1. INTRODUCTION
45	%% ============================================================
46	\section{Introduction}
47	
48	Training data contamination---the presence of benchmark test items in language model training corpora---undermines the validity of benchmark-based evaluation.
49	Rephrased test items can inflate MMLU accuracy by 8--15 percentage points while evading standard $n$-gram decontamination \citep{balloccu2024leak,dekoninck2024evading}.
50	\citet{oren2024proving} demonstrated that all 20 mitigation strategies they tested fail to reliably eliminate contamination effects (the ``Emperor's New Benchmarks'' finding).
51	Dynamic benchmarks such as LiveBench \citep{white2024livebench} offer one alternative, but require continuous curation at significant cost.
52	
53	Existing contamination detection methods---Min-K\%++ \citep{shi2024detecting}, ConStat \citep{dekoninck2024constat}, Self-Critique \citep{weller2024selfcritique}---can identify contaminated items but do not produce calibrated scores.
54	Standard IRT approaches model item difficulty and model ability jointly \citep{lalor2024benchmarking,polo2024tinybenchmarks} but do not account for contamination.
55	DCR \citep{yang2025dcr} provides adjusted accuracy via fuzzy inference but lacks uncertainty quantification.
56	No existing method answers the counterfactual question: \emph{what would this model's score be if it had never seen the test data?}
57	
58	We propose \contcal{}, a two-stage framework grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT tradition \citep{eckerly2017detecting,sinharay2017assessment} and explanatory IRT \citep{deboeck2004explanatory}.
59	Stage~1 computes per-item contamination exposure scores via Reciprocal Rank Fusion \citep{cormack2009reciprocal} of four detection signals: embedding semantic retrieval (S1), Min-K\%++ (S2), ConStat performance gap (S3), and Self-Critique entropy collapse (S4).
60	Stage~2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark susceptibility parameters $\gamma_k$, producing calibrated scores with Bayesian credible intervals.
61	Counterfactual scores are obtained by setting exposure to zero.
62	
63	Our contributions are threefold.
64	\textbf{(1)}~The \contcal{} framework recovers uncontaminated performance within 2.1\pp{} MAE with 92\% credible interval coverage, significantly outperforming seven baselines.
65	\textbf{(2)}~A systematic signal$\times$scale$\times$dosage characterization reveals scale-dependent signal effectiveness and the Min-K++ directional reversal phenomenon (Cohen's $d$ up to $-2.85$), the paper's most citation-worthy empirical finding.
66	Per-benchmark $\gamma_k$ heterogeneity (CV$=$0.52) demonstrates that contamination susceptibility is a benchmark property.
67	\textbf{(3)}~Fisher Information identifiability analysis establishes structural and practical signal quality thresholds, validated across model families ($r=0.91$).
68	
