71	%% 2. RELATED WORK
72	%% ============================================================
73	\section{Related Work}
74	
75	\paragraph{Contamination Detection.}
76	Membership inference approaches detect whether specific items appeared in training data.
77	Min-K\%++ \citep{shi2024detecting} identifies memorized items via low-perplexity token subsets, building on MIA foundations \citep{shokri2017membership,carlini2022quantifying}.
78	ConStat \citep{dekoninck2024constat} compares performance on original versus paraphrased items.
79	Self-Critique prompts models to assess their own familiarity \citep{weller2024selfcritique}.
80	KDS \citep{dong2024generalization} uses knowledge-dependent signals.
81	These methods flag contamination but do not produce calibrated scores.
82	
83	\paragraph{Contamination Mitigation.}
84	\citet{oren2024proving} systematically evaluated 20 mitigation strategies and found none effective, motivating post-hoc calibration.
85	Dynamic benchmarks \citep{white2024livebench} and human evaluation platforms \citep{zheng2023judging} bypass contamination but at high cost.
86	HELM \citep{liang2023holistic} provides standardized evaluation but does not address contamination.
87	
88	\paragraph{IRT for LLM Evaluation.}
89	Item Response Theory has been applied to LLM evaluation by several groups \citep{lalor2024benchmarking,polo2024tinybenchmarks}.
90	HiBayES provides Bayesian uncertainty but ignores contamination.
91	\citet{burnell2023rethink} explore multidimensional IRT for LLMs.
92	Our work extends this line by incorporating contamination covariates, connecting to the item compromise IRT literature in educational testing \citep{eckerly2017detecting,sinharay2017assessment} and Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis \citep{holland1993differential,thissen1993detection,zumbo1999handbook}.
93	
94	\paragraph{Post-hoc Calibration.}
95	DCR \citep{yang2025dcr} provides adjusted accuracy via fuzzy inference but lacks probabilistic uncertainty quantification.
96	Our $\gamma_k$ parameter is analogous to DIF between exposed and unexposed groups.
97	We note that ``calibration'' here refers to decontamination---correcting for systematic bias---not probability calibration in the sense of \citet{guo2017calibration} or \citet{platt1999probabilistic}.
98	Conformal prediction \citep{angelopoulos2023conformal} offers distribution-free coverage guarantees but assumes exchangeability violated by contamination's structured bias; Bayesian IRT naturally models this structure.
99	
100	\paragraph{Data Attribution.}
101	TracIn \citep{pruthi2020estimating} and TRAK \citep{park2023trak} attribute model predictions to training examples.
102	These could serve as additional contamination signals in future work.
